## Troubleshoot an App Service app by using Azure SRE Agent

## Prerequisites
# Make sure that your user account has the Microsoft.Authorization/roleAssignments/write permissions as either Role Based Access Control Administrator or User Access Administrator.

# Note: Regions that allow SRE are Australia East, East US 2, Sweden Central

## Create an App Service app
# Publish - Code, Runtime stack	- .NET 9 (STS), OS - Windows, Authentication settings - Basic

## Deploy the sample app
# Repo - https://github.com/Azure-Samples/app-service-dotnet-agent-tutorial
# Branch	main
# Source	External Git

## Verify the sample app
# The app displays a large counter and two buttons.
# Select the Increment button several times to observe the counter increase.

## Set up a deployment slot for failure simulation
# Add a slot.
# clone from the production slot

## Deploy the sample app to the slot
# Add same settings to deploy on slot from deployment center

## Add an app setting to enable error simulation
# In the environmental variables on the slot add INJECT_ERROR = 1
# Make sure that the Deployment slot setting box is not selected.

## Create an agent
# Create SRE agent. assign the resource group of the app service
# On the agent permission, i gave a priveledge permission rather than reader.

## Chat with your agent
# search your and select Azure SRE Agent on azure portal
# Select Chat with agent
# Enter prompt: List my App Service apps
# Now that the agent can see your app, you're ready to simulate a failure and let the agent help you resolve it.

## Break the app
# swap slots

## Fix the app
# Chat with agent.
# Enter prompt: What's wrong with [app service name]?
# The agent begins to analyze the app's health and ask for a rollback

## Verify the fix
# Notice that the text ERROR INJECTION ENABLED no longer appears

## Clean up resources.